./tests/util.rs:474:/// run our `rg` binary. We can't just run it directly since it might be
./tests/util.rs:475:/// compiled for a totally different target. Instead, it's likely that `cross`
./tests/util.rs:476:/// will have setup qemu to run it. While this is integrated into the Rust
./tests/util.rs:477:/// testing by default, we need to handle it ourselves for integration tests.
./tests/util.rs:478:///
./tests/util.rs:479:/// Now thankfully, cross sets `CROSS_RUNNER` to point to the right qemu
./tests/util.rs:480:/// executable. Or so one thinks. But it seems to always be set to `qemu-user`
./tests/util.rs:481:/// and I cannot find `qemu-user` anywhere in the Docker image. Awesome.
./tests/util.rs:482:///
./tests/util.rs:483:/// Thers is `/linux-runner` which seems to work sometimes? But not always.
./tests/util.rs:484:///
./tests/util.rs:485:/// Instead, it looks like we have to use `qemu-aarch64` in the `aarch64`
./tests/util.rs:486:/// case. Perfect, so just get the current target architecture and append it
./tests/util.rs:487:/// to `qemu-`. Wrong. Cross (or qemu or whoever) uses `qemu-ppc64` for
./tests/util.rs:488:/// `powerpc64`, so we can't just use the target architecture as Rust knows
./tests/util.rs:489:/// it verbatim.
./tests/util.rs:490:///
./tests/util.rs:491:/// So... we just manually handle these cases. So fucking fun.
